&lt;p&gt;Please join the Institute for the Study of Sexuality and Gender for &lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Mavericks: &lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A Book Salon on Race, Gender, &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Fashion &amp;amp; Avant-garde Culture in 1970s New York&lt;/strong&gt; with Professor Uri McMillan.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Wednesday, November 4th, 2026: 4-5:30pm&lt;br /&gt;
754 Schermerhorn Extension&lt;br /&gt;
No RSVP required unless you need Campus Access&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;[&lt;a href="https://www.eventbrite.com/e/mavericks-salon-on-race-gender-fashion-avant-garde-culture-in-1970-nyc-tickets-2003929523148" target="_blank"&gt;registration link for non-CU affiliates needing campus access&lt;/a&gt;]&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In &lt;em&gt;Mavericks of Style: &lt;/em&gt;&lt;em&gt;The Seventies in Color &lt;/em&gt;(Duke University Press, 2025)&lt;em&gt;,&lt;/em&gt; Uri McMillan tells the story of New York City&amp;rsquo;s downtown art and fashion scene of the 1970s through the lives and careers of experimental Black and Brown artists. McMillan focuses on model and musician Grace Jones, fashion illustrator Antonio Lopez, fashion designer Stephen Burrows, and their orbit of friends, showing how they restlessly moved across genres and disciplines, transgressing boundaries between the commercial and the avant-garde. Bypassing the exclusive art world and cultivating uniquely personal styles, these artists thrived on friendship and collaboration in their experimental use of bold color, gold lam&amp;eacute;, and Instamatic photography.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;In Collaboration with the &lt;a href="https://www.engineering.columbia.edu/" target="_blank"&gt;Engineering School&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;at Columbia University: Climate School Perspectives Dialogue: Powering Prosperity: Building Low-Carbon Energy Systems in Emerging Economies&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;5PM - Networking Welcome Reception; 6PM - Panel&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Program developed in collaboration with faculty partners&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://ccsi.columbia.edu/people/lisa-sachs/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt; Lisa Sachs&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;strong&gt; and&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.engineering.columbia.edu/faculty-staff/directory/greeshma-gadikota"&gt;&lt;strong&gt; Greeshma Gadikota.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Reliable, affordable energy is essential to economic development. Emerging economies need to expand energy systems that can support manufacturing, transportation, agriculture, cooling, and rising living standards. The choices made now about power generation, grids, and industry will shape economic opportunity and emissions for decades.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;No single technology can meet every need. Solar, wind, and batteries are changing energy systems, while countries also face questions about reliability, infrastructure, financing, and environmental impacts. How can governments build systems that support growth and energy security while moving toward a low-carbon future?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Join us for a discussion about the different strategies, timelines, and tradeoffs involved. Panelists will consider how countries can finance and build reliable energy systems, how to weigh cost and environmental impacts, and whether current climate and financing approaches are helping countries meet their goals.&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Webcast Details Coming Soon&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Opening remarks:&lt;/strong&gt; Shih-Fu Chang, Dean of Columbia Engineering&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Moderator:&lt;/strong&gt; Alexis M. Abramson, Dean of the Columbia Climate School&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Panelists:&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/juanjose80/" target="_blank"&gt;Juan Jose Martinez Badillo,&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;Chief, UN Trade and Development, New York Office, He services as the Chief of the UNCTAD New York Office and UNCTAD representative in New York since May 2023. Previously, he served as Chief of Regional Group Liaison at the UNCTAD Headquarters in Geneva, with responsibility for all matters related to the engagement of regional groups and individual members States with the intergovernmental machinery of UNCTAD and the work of UNCTAD at large.Mr. Martinez Badillo holds a degree in international relations from the Universidad Central de Venezuela as well as master&amp;rsquo;s degrees in international and development Economics&amp;nbsp;and in business administration.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/jamilwyne/" target="_blank"&gt;Jamil Wyne,&lt;/a&gt; Founder, Hazelwood Network -&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;&amp;nbsp;A&amp;nbsp;senior advisor at UNIDO, and was the lead author of the&lt;a href="https://urldefense.com/v3/__https://www.oxclimate.tech/the-climate-tech-opportunity__;!!BDUfV1Et5lrpZQ!VIBgDJ-sE3ou58_4OdAOp5On0iK84OgamMK0FImrgm8qC1bl8PiZCoH0S0ri-1wUT-R7mYvX8PjKKISD0HVIcOHyhY9nujsQjaA$"&gt; Climate Tech Opportunity&lt;/a&gt;, a global study from Oxford University. He has worked with the World Bank, IFC, UNICEF, the WWF, ClimateWorks Foundation, the EDF and governments. He has a &lt;a href="https://urldefense.com/v3/__https://www.linkedin.com/learning/climate-technology-for-business-resilience-and-adaptation/introduction-to-climate-technology-for-business-resilience__;!!BDUfV1Et5lrpZQ!VIBgDJ-sE3ou58_4OdAOp5On0iK84OgamMK0FImrgm8qC1bl8PiZCoH0S0ri-1wUT-R7mYvX8PjKKISD0HVIcOHyhY9nzqizypI$"&gt;Linkedin Learning course&lt;/a&gt; - Climate Technology for Business Resilience, and his work has been featured in Forbes, Stanford, Brookings, McKinsey, Bloomberg, Wall Street Journal, CNN, World Bank, and WEF publications. He has degrees from INSEAD, Johns Hopkins SAIS, and Bowdoin College, and has been a Fulbright, New American and Truman Fellow.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Join the Columbia University College of Dental Medicine for a presentation by &lt;strong&gt;Howard Israel, DDS &amp;rsquo;77&lt;/strong&gt;, Clinical Professor of Oral &amp;amp; Maxillofacial Surgery at Weill Cornell Medicine, exploring the ethical responsibilities of healthcare professionals and what happens when their ethical compass is compromised.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Dean Dennis A. Mitchell, DDS, MPH&lt;/strong&gt;, will deliver welcome remarks, and &lt;strong&gt;Sidney Eisig, DDS&lt;/strong&gt;, George Guttmann Professor of Clinical Craniofacial Surgery, Director of Oral &amp;amp; Maxillofacial Surgery, and Chair of Hospital Dentistry at Columbia Dental, will moderate the discussion.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Hosted by Columbia Dental&amp;rsquo;s Student Ethics and Professionalism Association in Dentistry, the event will examine the role of ethics in medical and dental practice and the lifelong responsibility of healthcare professionals to uphold the highest standards of patient care.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Wednesday, November 4, 2026&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
5:00 p.m. | Check-in and socializing&lt;br /&gt;
5:30&amp;ndash;6:30 p.m. | Presentation&lt;br /&gt;
