&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A Staged Reading and Conversation with Ga&amp;euml;l Faye&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Reading in French and English. Conversation in French&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Join us for an intimate evening with acclaimed Rwandan-French novelist and hip-hop artist Ga&amp;euml;l Faye as he presents his award-winning novel,&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://www.grasset.fr/auteur/gael-faye/" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;em&gt;Jacaranda&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&amp;mdash;a rich, deeply felt story of family, memory, and healing in post-genocide Rwanda.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The event will feature a bilingual staged reading of excerpts from&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;Jacaranda&lt;/em&gt; in French and English, performed by &lt;strong&gt;Ga&amp;euml;l Faye &lt;/strong&gt;and &lt;strong&gt;Brooke Habit&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;(PhD Candidate in French at Columbia University), featuring &lt;strong&gt;Sarah Ardizzone&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;rsquo;s translation (&lt;a href="https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/authors/2172505/gael-faye/" target="_blank"&gt;Penguin Random House&lt;/a&gt;). The reading will be followed by a conversation in French with the author, exploring the creative process, intergenerational memory, and history.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About the book:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;There is magic in Ga&amp;euml;l Faye&amp;rsquo;s writing . . . This book is unforgettable.&amp;rdquo;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&amp;mdash;Le&amp;iuml;la Slimani, internationally bestselling author of&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;The Perfect Nanny&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;Ga&amp;euml;l Faye&amp;rsquo;s talent is breathtaking.&amp;rdquo;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&amp;mdash;Imbolo Mbue, author of&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;Behold the Dreamers&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Milan&amp;mdash;the twelve-year-old son of a French father and a Rwandan mother&amp;mdash;blames flunking his exams on the emotional toll of the genocide in his mother&amp;rsquo;s homeland. In truth, his mother never talks about Rwanda; the violence is an abstraction that only reaches their French suburb through television broadcasts. That is, until Milan meets Claude, a small boy with a bandaged head whom Milan&amp;rsquo;s mother introduces as a cousin who has come to France seeking medical treatment. Milan embraces him as the brother he&amp;rsquo;s always wanted&amp;mdash;until, one day, Claude is sent back to Rwanda without warning, leaving him heartbroken and confused.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Four years later, the boys reunite as teenagers when Milan visits Rwanda for the first time with his mother in the wake of her divorce and discovers a more fractured and vibrant community than he could have imagined. But the trip raises more questions for him than it answers&amp;mdash;about family, the war, and its aftershocks. Over the course of many years, Milan will return to Rwanda again and again, compelled to unearth the secrets that have taken root in the shadows of long silences, confront the past, and imagine a new future.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This event is co-sponsored by Institute of African Studies, Institute of Comparative Literature and Society, Alliance Program, French Cultural Society, and the Maison Fran&amp;ccedil;aise at Columbia University.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Join us in the Burke Library&amp;rsquo;s main reading room (3rd floor) to celebrate the publication of &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://urldefense.com/v3/__https://arrow-journal.org/__;!!BDUfV1Et5lrpZQ!S9y7YfThepk86VeqlKP8a_7ILRvUZ3-vYnsP9U1VvC4FewLryNiJdd9K14lojXEY-R__LVNPpadBobd72ngGvfGZpKLFKg$" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;em&gt;The Arrow Journal&amp;#39;s&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt; &lt;/strong&gt;and Union Theological Seminary&amp;#39;s Buddhist Studies Program joint special issue, &amp;quot;Movement Dharma: Towards a Buddhist Movement Chaplaincy.&amp;quot;&amp;nbsp;This&amp;nbsp;volume includes essays from leading Buddhist scholars, practitioners, movement chaplains, and activists that help us consider what Buddhism might offer our current movements for justice and liberation, and also how Buddhists might organize to develop a more visionary and coherent public voice activism in these times of rupture and collapse.&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The event will be hosted by &lt;strong&gt;Brooke Lavelle&lt;/strong&gt;,&amp;nbsp;Union&amp;#39;s Buddhist Studies Program Manager and&amp;nbsp;Adjunct Faculty and Editor of &lt;em&gt;The Arrow Journal&lt;/em&gt;, along with &lt;strong&gt;Michelle (Mishka)&amp;nbsp;Erdenesanaa&lt;/strong&gt;, MDiv Student at Union and &lt;em&gt;The Arrow Journal&amp;#39;s&lt;/em&gt; Co-Editor. Special guest editor &lt;strong&gt;Monika Son&lt;/strong&gt;, and other contributing authors will also be present for brief readings and a discussion of the issue and its application.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
