&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;CTLgrads Learning Community - &lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The Good, the Bad, and the Ugly: The Role of Feelings and Emotions in the Classroom&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt; (Session 1) - for graduate students&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Teaching and learning have never been purely intellectual processes. Emotions in the classroom can be good, bad, and sometimes ugly, but they are always part of learning. Curiosity motivates students to engage with new ideas, joy fosters a sense of belonging, frustration can signal that a student needs additional support, and anxiety can make participation more difficult. Yet emotions are often treated as separate from, or even disruptive to, academic work. This Learning Community explores the role of feelings and emotions as an integral part of teaching and learning, for students and instructors alike. We will explore how emotional awareness can support engaged, inclusive, and rigorous teaching. Attendees will reflect on the emotions present in their own classrooms, consider how those emotions can facilitate or hinder learning, and develop practical strategies for responding to them intentionally.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;This is the first session of two-part Learning Community is in-person&lt;/strong&gt; and will be co-facilitated by Anna Leigh Garner and Juan Camilo Rojas Gomez, Senior Lead Teaching Fellows at the Center for Teaching and Learning Community. Participants should register for and attend &lt;strong&gt;both&lt;/strong&gt; sessions of this LC.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;In Session 1&lt;/strong&gt;, attendees will focus on the emotions students bring into the classroom. We will begin by reflecting on our own experiences of emotion as learners, then consider how particular emotions such as curiosity, joy, anxiety, frustration, embarrassment, and fear shape students&amp;#39; willingness to participate, take intellectual risks, persist through challenges, and connect with their peers and instructors. When does frustration become productive, and when does it become a barrier? How might an instructor tell whether a moment calls for pedagogical adjustment, additional clarity, reassurance, or simply a little more time? Through short classroom scenarios, such as a student frustrated with an assignment, a student anxious about participating, or conflict emerging during a discussion, participants will develop a flexible repertoire of responses they can adapt to different students, courses, and contexts. Taking students&amp;#39; emotions seriously does not mean lowering academic expectations or asking instructors to become counselors. Rather, it means recognizing that students&amp;#39; capacity to learn is shaped by whether they feel respected, supported, and able to take intellectual risks.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Session 1 will convene on Friday, 11/06, 11:40-12:55pm in room 212 Butler Library.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Session 2 will convene on Friday, 11/13, 11:40-12:55pm in room 212 Butler Library; registration is available &lt;a href="https://events.columbia.edu/cal/event/eventView.do?b=de&amp;amp;calPath=%2Fpublic%2Fcals%2FMainCal&amp;amp;guid=CAL-00bbdb70-a0e70b2f-01a0-e95ea8c2-000056a1events@columbia.edu&amp;amp;recurrenceId="&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Each session of this LC counts as a Pedagogy Workshop for the&lt;a href="https://tdp.ctl.columbia.edu/"&gt; &lt;strong&gt;Teaching Development Program (TDP)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. See the&lt;a href="https://tdp.ctl.columbia.edu/about/faqs/"&gt; &lt;strong&gt;TDP FAQs&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; for more information about obtaining credit for this and other graduate student-led events through the CTL.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

