&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Registration REQUIRED by 4pm on November 5, 2026 in order to attend this event.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Please join the Harriman Institute for a lecture by&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Vlad Strukov&lt;/strong&gt;. Moderated by&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://harriman.columbia.edu/person/mark-lipovetsky/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mark Lipovetsky&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Vlad Strukov will use gender and sexuality as critical lenses through which to examine how international, institutional and independent actors participate in the ongoing renegotiation of normativity across politics, society and culture, taking Azerbaijan as a point of departure. The concept of the post-atheist society will provide a framework for rethinking established theoretical debates around religion, spirituality, secularism and emancipation, drawing on Derrida and others.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In particular, he will ask how forms of belonging are articulated in Muslim-majority secular cultures whose histories complicate conventional distinctions between the religious and the secular, tradition and emancipation, and, subsequently, normativity. Strukov&amp;#39;s analysis of Azerbaijan&amp;rsquo;s experience will challenge and complicate &amp;quot;national&amp;quot; accounts of modernity and neoliberal forms of contemporaneity.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Strukov will approach visual culture as a key arena in which these tensions are negotiated, contested and made legible. He will analyze visual discourses and narratives across a broad range of sites, including major museums, urban projects, film festivals, cultural events and other texts, in order to trace how institutions and cultural actors participate in the production, disruption and reconfiguration of the norms.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;More broadly, Strukov&amp;#39;s research seeks to challenge theoretical models of secular modernity that have been developed primarily through Western European, transatlantic histories. By foregrounding the experience of post-atheist, Muslim-majority societies, it asks how our understanding of secularism, belonging and emancipation might change when these concepts are approached from locations shaped simultaneously by Soviet atheism, Muslim cultural histories, and globalization.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The research underpinning this presentation is supported by a major international AHRC-DFG grant involving researchers from the Universities of Leeds in the UK and Regensburg in Germany. Vlad Strukov is the principal investigator of the project. The presentation will be also of interest to those who wish to develop research collaborations with European universities and learn more about funding opportunities in the UK and beyond.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In collaboration with the&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://www.centralparknyc.org/"&gt;Central Park Conservancy&lt;/a&gt;, join University Life to learn about the history of Seneca Village!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Before Central Park was created, the landscape along what is now the Park&amp;rsquo;s perimeter from West 82nd to West 89th Street was the site of &lt;a href="https://www.centralparknyc.org/seneca-village" target="_blank"&gt;Seneca Village&lt;/a&gt;,&amp;nbsp;a&amp;nbsp;thriving,&amp;nbsp;predominantly&amp;nbsp;Black&amp;nbsp;community&amp;nbsp;where&amp;nbsp;many&amp;nbsp;residents built businesses and owned&amp;nbsp;property. The community was displaced&amp;nbsp;through NYC legislative action in the 1860s to make way for Central Park.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The tour will consist of a guided walk to notable community locations, a timeline of the village, and visits to plaques and land markers that still exist today. Before the tour, you&amp;rsquo;ll receive a small notebook to write down facts, thoughts, and questions. At the end of the program, you&amp;rsquo;ll gather with fellow participants to share your reflections.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This event is&amp;nbsp;part of the &lt;a href="https://universitylife.columbia.edu/CCI"&gt;Community Citizenship Initiative&lt;/a&gt; (CCI) and&amp;nbsp;is designed for first semester students who want to develop skills grounded in self-awareness and inclusivity. Students attending&amp;nbsp;will receive credit for CCI and will have their requirement considered complete.&lt;/p&gt;
