&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Registration REQUIRED by 4pm on November 6, 2026 to attend this event.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Please join the Harriman Institute for a lecture by&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Nina Khrushcheva&lt;/strong&gt;. Moderated by&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://harriman.columbia.edu/person/timothy-m-frye/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Timothy Frye&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Since 2022, the Kremlin has worked to recast its invasion of Ukraine as a national &amp;quot;special military operation,&amp;quot; using state media, a revived cult of Stalin, and the militarization of everyday life. Yet five years on, when the distant &amp;quot;operation&amp;quot; in Ukraine turned into the &amp;quot;war&amp;quot; that only a few in Russia can ignore, Putin&amp;rsquo;s propaganda has shown only partial success. Many Russians, especially the young, still find independent information, and make an effort of a quiet normal life that goes on largely apart from the war. Drawing on her frequent travels across Russia during these years, Nina Khrushcheva examines where the Kremlin&amp;rsquo;s message has taken hold, where it has failed, and what that might mean for Russia after Putin.&lt;/p&gt;

